# MLIR Optimization Log: matmul_128_256_128
# Started: 2026-02-26 02:16
# Shape: M=128, K=256, N=128 (f64)
# Target CPU: Intel Xeon E5-2680 v4 (Broadwell, AVX2, 4 doubles/vector, 28 cores)

## Setup
- L1: 32KB/core, L2: 256KB/core, L3: 35MB shared
- AVX2: 4x f64 per vector
- Matrix sizes: A=128x256, B=256x128, C=128x128

## Phase 1: Baseline Measurement
- MLIR baseline: 6.184 ms (success=True)
- PyTorch baseline: 0.035 ms
- PyTorch is ~177x faster than MLIR baseline
- Note: Added --qos=c2 to mlir.sh and torch.sh to bypass AssocGrpCpuLimit

## Phase 2: Optimization Candidates

### C2: forall[16,16] + tile_for[0,0,32] (parallel only, no vectorize)
- Result: 0.132 ms | Speedup vs baseline: 47x | vs PyTorch: 0.26x

### C3: forall[16,16] + tile_for[0,0,32] + tile_for[4,4,4] + vectorize[4,4,4]
- vector<4x4x4xf64>=64 elements (rank-3 very small, allowed)
- Result: 0.074 ms | Speedup vs baseline: 84x | vs PyTorch: 0.47x

### C4: forall[8,16] + tile_for[0,0,32] + tile_for[4,4,4] + vectorize[4,4,4]
- Same vectorization but 128 parallel blocks (vs 64) for better 28-core utilization
- Result: 0.052 ms | Speedup vs baseline: 118x | vs PyTorch: 0.67x

### C5: forall[8,16] + tile_for[0,0,32] only (no vectorize)
- Control: verifies explicit vectorize is necessary
- Result: 0.128 ms | Confirms vectorize is critical

### C6: forall[8,16] + tile_for[0,0,64] + tile_for[4,4,4] + vectorize[4,4,4]
- K=64 pre-tile variant
- Result: 0.055 ms | Worse than K=32

### C7: C4 code + innerreduction contraction strategy → FAILED (couldn't parse result)
### C7b: C4 code + innerparallel contraction strategy → FAILED

### C8: forall[8,16] + tile_for[0,0,32] + tile_for[2,8,4] + vectorize[2,8,4]
- vector<2x8x4xf64>=64 elements alternative
- Result: 0.052 ms | Same as C4

### C9: forall[8,16] + tile_for[4,4,4] (no K pre-tile) + vectorize[4,4,4]
- Without K pre-tile: cache thrashing
- Result: 0.061 ms | Worse — K pre-tiling is crucial

### C10: forall[8,16] + tile_for[0,0,32] + tile_for[1,16,4] + vectorize[1,16,4]
- Wider N vector attempt
- Result: 0.067 ms | Worse

### C11: forall[8,8] + tile_for[0,0,32] + tile_for[4,4,4] + vectorize[4,4,4]
- 256 parallel blocks (16x16) for better 28-core utilization
- Result: 0.048 ms | Speedup vs baseline: 128x | vs PyTorch: 0.73x  ← BEST

### C12: forall[8,8] + tile_for[0,0,16] + tile_for[4,4,4] + vectorize[4,4,4]
- K=16 variant
- Result: 0.057 ms | Worse than K=32

### C13: forall[8,8] + tile_for[0,0,64] + tile_for[4,4,4] + vectorize[4,4,4]
- K=64 variant
- Result: 0.051 ms | Worse than K=32 — confirms K=32 optimal

### C14b: forall[8,8] + K[32] + [4,4,4] + generalize + interchange[0,2,1] + vectorize[4,4,4]
- Loop interchange m-k-n (K loop outside N loop)
- Result: 0.052 ms | Slightly worse than C11

### C15: forall[4,4] + K[32] + vectorize[4,4,32]
- 1024 parallel blocks; vector<4x4x32xf64>=512 elements
- Result: 0.059 ms | Too many blocks + register pressure

### C16: forall[4,8] + K[32] + [4,4,4] + vectorize[4,4,4]
- 512 parallel blocks (32x16)
- Result: 0.058 ms | Still worse than 256-block sweet spot

## Phase 3: Final Results

### Parallelism Sweep Summary (all with K[32] + [4,4,4] + vectorize)
| Blocks | forall | Time |
|--------|--------|------|
| 64     | [16,16] | 0.074ms |
| 128    | [8,16]  | 0.052ms |
| **256** | **[8,8]** | **0.048ms** ← BEST |
| 512    | [4,8]   | 0.058ms |
| 1024   | [4,4]   | 0.059ms |

### Winner: Candidate 11
- Schedule: tile_using_forall [8,8] + tile_for [0,0,32] + tile_for [4,4,4] + vectorize [4,4,4]
- MLIR baseline: 6.184 ms
- Optimized: 0.048 ms
- Speedup vs baseline: 128x
- Speedup vs PyTorch: 0.73x (1.37x slower than PyTorch)
